200 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 95 0 R
/E (The results show that the models that use techniques like Random Forest and XGBoost did better than the simpler models, like Logistic Regression and Decision Tree. This is because the student dropout prediction problem is complex and needs models that can handle different factors.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [47]
>>
endobj
201 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 95 0 R
/E (Among the models Logistic Regression did pretty well with an accuracy of 86.34% and a recall of 81.27%.. Decision Tree did not do as well especially when it came to recall \(72.28%\) and ROC-AUC \(80.41%\). This suggests that using one decision tree is not enough to capture all the complexity of student learning behavior.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [48]
>>
endobj
202 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 95 0 R
/E (The models that use techniques like Random Forest and XGBoost did better. Random Forest improved recall to 84.19%. Xgboost got the highest ROC-AUC \(94.97%\) among the untuned models. This shows that these models are good at distinguishing between students who're likely to continue their studies and those who are at risk of dropping out.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [49]
>>
endobj
203 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 95 0 R
/E (When we tuned the models they got better. The Tuned Random Forest got the overall accuracy \(87.59%\) and the Tuned XGBoost model got the highest recall \(89.72%\). This is important because it shows that there is a trade-off between getting the accuracy right and being able to identify students who are really at risk of dropping out.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [50]
>>
endobj
204 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 95 0 R
/E (From a perspective recall is the most important metric because we want to find as many students at risk of dropping out as possible. If we miss a student it can be bad because they might not get the help they need.. If we incorrectly identify a student who is not at risk it is not as bad.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [51]
>>
endobj
205 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 95 0 R
/E (So even though the Tuned Random Forest got a higher accuracy the Tuned XGBoost model was better for our purposes because it was better at identifying students who needed help.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [52]
>>
endobj
206 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 95 0 R
/E (4.1 Experimental Setup)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [53]
>>
endobj
207 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 95 0 R
/E (To see how well the student dropout prediction framework works we did some experiments using the Open University Learning Analytics Dataset. We prepared the data. Then split it into two parts: one for training and one for testing. We made sure that the two parts had the balance of students who dropped out and those who did not. We used the setup to train and test all the machine learning models so that we could compare them fairly.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [54]
>>
endobj
208 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 95 0 R
/E (We looked at six machine learning models: Logistic Regression, Decision Tree, Random Forest, XGBoost Tuned Random Forest and Tuned XGBoost. The first four models gave us a baseline to compare to and the last two models were tuned to see if they could do better. We used a technique called GridSearchCV to find the best settings for the Random Forest and XGBoost models.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [55]
>>
endobj
209 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 95 0 R
/E (We measured how each model did using five different metrics: Accuracy, Precision, Recall, F1-score and ROC-AUC. Even though we looked at all five metrics Recall was the important one because we wanted to find as many students at risk of dropping out as possible. If we miss a student who's at risk it can be bad because they might not get the help they need.. If we incorrectly identify a student who is not at risk it is not as bad.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [56]
>>
endobj
210 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 95 0 R
/E (The results of our experiments are presented in the subsection, where we compare the strengths and weaknesses of each model.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [57]
>>
endobj
