/Title (2.3 Ensemble Learning for Student Dropout Prediction)
/Dest [11 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 667 0 R
/Next 673 0 R
>>
endobj
672 0 obj
<<
/Title (VI. CONCLUSION AND FUTURE WORK)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 674 0 R
/Next 668 0 R
>>
endobj
673 0 obj
<<
/Title (To make predictions better researchers started using learning. This means combining the predictions of models to get a more accurate result. Ensemble models are better at predicting dropouts because they can handle relationships between different variables.Tree-based ensemble models like Gradient Boosting and XGBoost are particularly good at this. They look at demographic and behavioral variables all together. When we combine learning with careful preparation of the data the predictions get even better. Ensemble models are more stable and reliable than models but they are also more complex. This makes it hard for teachers to understand how the predictions are made.So researchers are now looking at ways to make these models more explainable.)
/Dest [11 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 671 0 R
/Next 675 0 R
>>
endobj
674 0 obj
<<
/Title (Beeswarm plot)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 676 0 R
/Next 672 0 R
>>
endobj
675 0 obj
<<
/Title (2.4 Explainable Artificial Intelligence in Student Dropout Prediction)
/Dest [11 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 673 0 R
/Next 677 0 R
>>
endobj
676 0 obj
<<
/Title (Overall the SHAP analysis shows that the XGBoost model is not good at making predictions but also at explaining why it makes those predictions. This helps teachers trust the models recommendations and make decisions about how to help students. The XGBoost. Shap work together to provide a clear understanding of which students are at risk and why. This is very useful, for schools and teachers who want to help students succeed.)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 678 0 R
/Next 674 0 R
>>
endobj
677 0 obj
<<
/Title (As the models get more complex it becomes more important to understand how they make predictions. Even if a model is very accurate schools need to know why it is making predictions so they can make good decisions. Explainable Artificial Intelligence is a way to do this.There are techniques like LIME and SHAP that help us understand models. LIME looks at how the model works for a student and SHAP looks at how each feature affects the prediction.)
/Dest [11 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 675 0 R
/Next 679 0 R
>>
endobj
678 0 obj
<<
/Title (We also looked at how individual features affect the models predictions. For example we looked at how the last day a student was active affects the prediction. This helps us understand how student behavior affects the models predictions.)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 680 0 R
/Next 676 0 R
>>
endobj
679 0 obj
<<
/Title (These techniques help us understand the predictions better and make informed decisions. There is still a lot of work to be done to make models that are both accurate and explainable.)
/Dest [11 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 677 0 R
/Next 681 0 R
>>
endobj
680 0 obj
<<
/Title (To get an understanding of individual students we used SHAP waterfall and force plots. These plots break down the prediction into parts and show which features increase or decrease the risk of dropping out. This helps teachers understand why a student is at risk. This is very important in schools because teachers need to know why a student is struggling so they can help them.)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 682 0 R
/Next 678 0 R
>>
endobj
681 0 obj
<<
/Title (2.5 Research Gap)
/Dest [11 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 679 0 R
/Next 683 0 R
>>
endobj
682 0 obj
<<
/Title (We used SHAP to look at the model and see how each feature contributes to the predictions. We made some plots to visualize the data. These plots show how different features like behavior academics and demographics affect the models predictions. The model does not just look at one thing it looks at features to estimate the risk of a student dropping out. This means that dropping out is not just because of one thing but because of things.)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 684 0 R
/Next 680 0 R
>>
endobj
683 0 obj
<<
/Title (A lot of progress has been made in using Learning Analytics, machine learning, ensemble learning and Explainable Artificial Intelligence to predict student dropouts.. There are still some big challenges. The predictions are not always accurate. This can depend on how the data is prepared and analyzed.)
/Dest [11 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 681 0 R
/Next 685 0 R
>>
endobj
684 0 obj
<<
/Title (The XGBoost model is really good at finding students who're at risk of dropping out.. It is also very important to understand why the model makes certain predictions. This is especially true for schools and teachers who want to help students. To make the model more transparent we used something called SHapley Additive exPlanations or SHAP for short. SHAP helps us see how each feature affects the prediction.)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 686 0 R
/Next 682 0 R
>>
endobj
685 0 obj
<<
/Title (Also many studies focus either on making predictions or on making the models explainable but not on doing both.)
/Dest [11 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 683 0 R
/Next 687 0 R
>>
endobj
686 0 obj
<<
/Title (By understanding how the model works we can use it to identify students who're, at risk of dropping out and provide them with the help they need. This is the goal of our student dropout prediction framework and the Tuned XGBoost model is a part of it.)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 688 0 R
/Next 684 0 R
>>
endobj
687 0 obj
<<
/Title (This means we need an approach that combines accurate predictions with explanations that teachers can understand. This study is trying to do that by using XGBoost to make predictions and SHAP to explain how the features affect the predictions. The goal is to make a model that predicts dropouts accurately and also helps teachers understand why certain students are, at risk.)
/Dest [11 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 685 0 R
/Next 689 0 R
>>
endobj
688 0 obj
<<
/Title (We used a technique called SHAP to understand how the Tuned XGBoost model makes its predictions. This helps us trust the model and use it to make decisions. We looked at how the different factors, like characteristics, assessment outcomes and behavioral engagement influenced the models predictions.)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 690 0 R
/Next 686 0 R
>>
endobj
689 0 obj
<<
/Title (The goal of the proposed methodology is to find out the students who are at risk of dropping out at an early stage by analysing their, academic performance, assessment records, registration history, and online learning behaviour. The complete workflow consists of six major stages: data collection, preprocessing, feature engineering, model development, performance evaluation, and model interpretation using Explainable Artificial Intelligence \(XAI\). The overall workflow of the proposed framework is illustrated in Figure 1.)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 687 0 R
/Next 691 0 R
>>
endobj
690 0 obj
<<
/Title (4.5 Explainability Analysis Using SHAP)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 692 0 R
/Next 688 0 R
>>
endobj
691 0 obj
<<
/Title (A.DATASET DISCRIPTION:- We used the Open University Learning Analytics Dataset \(OULAD\), a publicly available dataset which is widely used in educational data mining and learning analytics research. The dataset contains detailed information about students enrolled in online courses, including, course registrations, assessment performance, Virtual Learning Environment \(VLE\) , and final academic outcomes. The combination of academic and behavioural information makes OULAD well suited for developing predictive models for early student dropout detection.)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 689 0 R
/Next 693 0 R
>>
endobj
